Native TOOM

This directory is a new TempleOS implementation, not a claim of authorship
by Terry A. Davis and not an unmodified release of id Software's engine.

TMDATA.BIN and TmData.HH contain state, actor, weapon, sprite, sound, integer
angle tables, gameplay identifiers and deterministic random data derived from
id Software's DOOM source:

  Copyright (C) 1993-1996 id Software, Inc.
  https://github.com/id-Software/DOOM
  revision a77dfb96cb91780ca334d0d4cfd86957558007e0

The data was converted to a compact native format and patched with Freedoom's
five full-bright firing-frame and two shotgun-flash duration changes on
2026-11-11. It is not the unmodified original. The conversion source is tools/toom/Data.mjs; pinned
source paths and SHA-256 digests are in tools/toom/source/source.json.
That tool retrieves and verifies the corresponding original source, keeping
its notices intact. No original C runtime, platform shim or network code is
linked into the TempleOS implementation.

Map collision, wall sliding, fixed-tic actor motion, aiming/shot queries,
damage/death handling, inventory, hit effects, player projectiles, weapon
control, fixed-tic player movement/view/power/death, actor sight and blast
damage, moving-plane clipping/crushing, door/floor/lift/ceiling controllers and activation, teleport movement/effects, switch texture/button state,
hit cross-sections and axis/vertex conventions were re-expressed in
native HolyC from p_map.c, p_maputl.c, p_mobj.c, p_inter.c, p_pspr.c,
p_user.c, p_sight.c, p_floor.c, p_doors.c, p_plats.c, p_ceilng.c, p_telept.c,
the door/neighbor/donut portions of
p_spec.c, switch texture/button portions of p_switch.c, constants from
p_spec.h, and the
three double-shotgun sound actions in p_enemy.c
at the same id Software revision. Reference paths and digests are in
tools/toom/source/collision.json.
The native path collector uses exact integer intersections, complete grid
edge/corner traversal and deterministic wall-first ties. Motion splits fast
moves symmetrically, preserves odd fixed-point deltas and slides only the
uncommitted remainder. It is not a demo-synchronization claim.

Integer trigonometry preserves the original tables.c samples, including the
cosine extension. Native fixed division uses defined saturation at signed
limits, and angle slope evaluation uses a wide numerator to avoid legacy
32-bit overflow for distant points.

